<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Gurjaras of Nandipuri</h1><p class="st-meta">580 CE – 738 CE (approx.) · capital Nandipuri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Gurjara dynasty of southern Gujarat around Bharuch that fought Arab raiders in the 8th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A Gurjara family established itself in the late 6th century in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lata-region">Lata</a> region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a> and Nandipuri, probably near modern Nandod. Its founder was Dadda I, and the line is known entirely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate land grants.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kings ruled a small but wealthy territory around the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a>, usually as feudatories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalachuris-of-mahishmati">Kalachuris of Mahishmati</a> and then of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadda-ii">Dadda II</a> is credited with protecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitraka-dynasty">Maitraka</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valabhi">Valabhi</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>. The dynasty patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and Sun worship, according to its grants.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last known king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayabhata-iv">Jayabhata IV</a>, fought Arab raids from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in the 730s. The dynasty ends around 738, probably overwhelmed by the Arab incursions and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lata-region">Lata</a> passed later to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 580 CE – 738 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:71.24%;width:3.14%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>580 CE – 738 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nandipuri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dadda II, Jayabhata IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gurjaras of Lata, Gurjaras of Broach</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=659&amp;focus=gurjaras-of-nandipuri" aria-label="Gurjaras of Nandipuri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6781 1355 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gurjaras of Nandipuri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7097 1795h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7101 1611h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7101 1611h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=659&amp;focus=gurjaras-of-nandipuri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

